# Inertia + Alba + Inertia Cable Architecture

## Key Conversion Pipeline

Frontend sends camelCase → Rails receives snake_case → Alba responds in camelCase.

| Layer | Mechanism | Direction |
|-------|-----------|-----------|
| **Incoming params** | `ApplicationController#underscore_params` (`deep_transform_keys!(&:underscore)`) | camelCase → snake_case |
| **Inertia props** | `InertiaRails.config.prop_transformer` (`camelize(:lower)`) | snake_case → camelCase |
| **Alba resources** | `ApplicationResource` has `transform_keys :lower_camel` | snake_case → camelCase |
| **Typelizer types** | `properties_transformer` (`camelize(:lower)`) | snake_case → camelCase |

**CRITICAL:** `wrap_parameters false` is set on `Web::ApplicationController`. Without it, Rails `ParamsWrapper` auto-adds an empty `terminal_session: {}` key that collides with the underscored `terminalSession` key after `deep_transform_keys!`.

## Serialization: Alba Resources Only

All serialization goes through Alba resources (`ApplicationResource` subclasses). **Never** use `ActiveModel::Serializers`, `as_json`, or inline hashes for Inertia props.

```ruby
# ✅ Inertia page
render inertia: "Sessions/ShowPage", props: {
  session: TerminalSessionResource.new(session).to_h,
  cable_stream: inertia_cable_stream(session)
}

# ✅ JSON API (same resource)
render json: TerminalSessionResource.new(session).to_h

# ❌ NEVER — bypass type generation, keys not transformed
render json: session.as_json(only: [:id, :state])
render inertia: "Page", props: { session: { id: session.id, state: session.state } }
```

Computed attributes that depend on `Settings` or URL helpers belong **in the resource**, not in the controller:

```ruby
# ✅ In resource
attribute :websocket_url do |session|
  next nil unless session.route_token.present?
  "#{Settings.traefik.ws_base}/t/#{session.route_token}/tty/ws"
end

# ❌ NEVER — manual URL assembly in controller
session_props = SomeResource.new(session).to_h
session_props[:websocket_url] = "#{Settings.traefik.ws_base}/..."
```


## ⚠️ CRITICAL: Wrap Hash/Array Props in Lambdas

**When a page has ANY `InertiaRails.defer` prop, ALL eager Hash/Array props MUST be wrapped in `-> { ... }`.**

Plain Hash values are recursively traversed by `PropsResolver` during partial reloads (deferred prop fetch). Scalar keys get filtered out while Array keys leak through — producing a corrupted partial object.

```ruby
# ❌ BROKEN — plain Hash corrupted during deferred prop partial reload
render inertia: "Page", props: {
  record: RecordResource.new(record).to_h,
  heavy: InertiaRails.defer { expensive },
}

# ✅ CORRECT — lambda makes each prop atomic during partial reload
render inertia: "Page", props: {
  record: -> { RecordResource.new(record).to_h },
  heavy: InertiaRails.defer { expensive },
}
```

See `inertia-drawer-data-pattern` rule for the full technical explanation.

## Data Loading: Props First, Never Custom Fetch

**Always prefer Inertia props over client-side `fetch()`.** Data should flow from the controller through props, not be fetched by the component after mount.

```ruby
# ✅ Controller loads all data the page needs
render inertia: "Onboarding/OnboardingPage", props: {
  auth_sessions: current_user.terminal_sessions.auth_sessions.active
                   .map { |s| TerminalSessionResource.new(s).to_h }
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ Component reads data from props, cable refreshes props
const { session } = usePage().props;
useInertiaCableStream(session?.cableStream, { only: ['session'] });

// ❌ NEVER — local state + fetch to mirror server data
const [session, setSession] = useState(null);
const fetchSession = async (id) => {
  const res = await fetch(`/terminal_sessions/${id}`);
  setSession(await res.json());
};
```

When a record must be created client-side (e.g. POST), reload props after creation instead of storing the response in local state:

```tsx
// ✅ POST then reload props — component stays driven by server data
const res = await fetch('/terminal_sessions', { method: 'POST', ... });
if (res.ok) router.reload({ only: ['authSessions'] });

// ❌ NEVER — parse response into local state, duplicate server truth
const data = await res.json();
setSessionId(data.id);
setSessionState(data.state);
setCableStream(data.cableStream);
```

## Resource Layering: Lean List vs Full Detail

When the same model appears both in a list (board cards) and a detail view (sidebar), use two resources:

```ruby
# BoardTaskResource — lean, for board card list
class BoardTaskResource < ApplicationResource
  attributes :id, :title, :task_type, :priority, :assignee_id, :board_column_id,
             :position, :parent_task_id, :tags, :created_at, :updated_at

  attribute :assignee_name do |task|
    task.assignee&.name
  end

  # Read SQL-precomputed count virtual attributes (avoids N+1 + Bullet warnings)
  attribute :comments_count do |task|
    task.has_attribute?(:comments_count) ? task[:comments_count].to_i : task.task_comments.size
  end

  attribute :children_count do |task|
    task.has_attribute?(:children_count) ? task[:children_count].to_i : task.child_tasks.size
  end

  # Sort in-memory using includes-loaded association — never call .order on it (triggers new query)
  attribute :recent_workflow_runs do |task|
    task.workflow_runs.sort_by(&:created_at).last(5).reverse.map do |run|
      { id: run.id, state: run.state, created_at: run.created_at.iso8601 }
    end
  end
end

# TaskDetailResource — extends with sidebar-only fields
class TaskDetailResource < BoardTaskResource
  attribute :description do |task|
    task.description
  end

  attribute :assets_count do |task|
    task.task_assets.size
  end
end
```

In the controller, inject count subqueries into `select` so the list query doesn't load full associations just to count them:

```ruby
board.board_tasks
     .select(Arel.sql(<<~SQL))
       board_tasks.*,
       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM task_comments WHERE board_task_id = board_tasks.id) AS comments_count,
       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM board_tasks children WHERE children.parent_task_id = board_tasks.id) AS children_count
     SQL
     .includes(:assignee, :workflow_runs, :pending_task_waits)
     .order(:position).map { |t| BoardTaskResource.new(t).to_h }
```

## Real-Time: Inertia Cable Only

All real-time updates use `inertia_cable` gem. **No custom ActionCable channels.**

### Backend: Model

```ruby
after_commit :broadcast_updates, on: :update

def broadcast_updates
  broadcast_refresh_to(self)
end
```

For child → parent propagation, use `touch`:
```ruby
# child model
after_commit :touch_parent, on: :update
def touch_parent = parent.touch  # triggers parent's after_commit → broadcast_refresh_to
```

**Board cable scope:** the cable subscribes to the **Board** object (not individual tasks). Any task mutation calls `board.touch` → `Board#after_commit` → `broadcast_refresh_to(board)`. The cable `only` list should include **only board-level props**: `['tasks', 'columns', 'recent_activities']`. Sidebar detail props (`selectedTask`, `taskComments`, `taskAssets`, etc.) are **not** refreshed via cable — they are refreshed by explicit `router.reload({ only: [...] })` after mutations.

### Backend: Controller

Pass `cable_stream` prop for pages that need live updates:
```ruby
render inertia: "ShowPage", props: {
  record: RecordResource.new(record).to_h,
  cable_stream: inertia_cable_stream(record)
}
```

### Frontend: Page Component

```tsx
import { useInertiaCableStream } from 'shared/lib/hooks/useInertiaCableStream';

const { record, cableStream } = usePage().props;
useInertiaCableStream(cableStream, {
  only: ['record'],           // reload only these props
  enabled: !isTerminalState,  // stop when record reaches final state
});
```

When `cableStream` is nested inside a serialized record (not a top-level prop), read it from the record:

```tsx
const { session } = usePage().props;
useInertiaCableStream(session?.cableStream, {
  only: ['session'],
  enabled: session && !isTerminal(session.state),
});
```

### Anti-Patterns

```ruby
# ❌ Custom ActionCable channel
class MyChannel < ApplicationCable::Channel; end

# ❌ Broadcasting to custom channel
MyChannel.broadcast_to(record, payload)

# ❌ update_column without broadcast (bypasses after_commit)
record.update_column(:field, value)  # silent — no broadcast_refresh_to triggered
```

```tsx
// ❌ Client-side fetch to read data that should be in props
const [data, setData] = useState(null);
useEffect(() => { fetch('/endpoint').then(r => r.json()).then(setData); }, []);

// ❌ Polling with setInterval
const interval = setInterval(fetchData, 3000);

// ❌ Manual ActionCable subscription for data that props can deliver
consumer.subscriptions.create({ channel: 'InertiaCable::StreamChannel', ... },
  { received(data) { fetchRecord(id); } });
// (use useInertiaCableStream hook instead — it calls router.reload automatically)
```

If you must use `update_column`, call `broadcast_refresh_to` explicitly after:
```ruby
record.update_column(:metadata, new_meta)
record.broadcast_refresh_to(record)
```
